WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Managed memory still needs deliberate design.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:16.280
Garbage collection helps reclaim memory, but it does not prevent an application from allocating too much, retaining objects too long, buffering unbounded input, or building caches that grow beyond their useful purpose.

3
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.260
Memory behavior affects performance, availability, and data exposure.

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:29.620
Large objects, repeated allocations, broad object graphs, retained references, and large request bodies can create memory pressure.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:37.640
A service may continue working for a while and then degrade as collections become more expensive, queues grow, or requests compete for memory.

6
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.220
The safest design avoids loading more data than the operation needs.

7
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:51.540
Object pools and array pools can reduce allocation costs in some workflows, but pooled resources create their own responsibilities.

8
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:58.440
Buffers must be returned reliably, should not be used after return, and may need clearing when they hold sensitive data.

9
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.940
Pooling should be measured and reviewed rather than applied everywhere by habit.

10
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.900
Caches are useful when they have boundaries.

11
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.800
A cache should define size limits, expiration behavior, invalidation rules, tenant separation, and what data is appropriate to retain.

12
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:23.580
A cache that never evicts or mixes data across boundaries can turn a performance optimization into an operational and data-safety problem.

13
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.660
Queues and channels need bounds or backpressure when producers can outpace consumers.

14
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:37.320
Without capacity planning, background work can hide pressure until memory, latency, or dependency capacity becomes the visible failure.

15
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:43.900
Bounded queues, rejection behavior, batching, and worker limits make pressure visible earlier.

16
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:50.820
Streaming can reduce unnecessary buffering when the application does not need all data in memory at once.

17
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.320
It is most useful when paired with size limits, cancellation, cleanup, and validation.

18
00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.580
The goal is to process data predictably without retaining sensitive or oversized content longer than necessary.
